1
00:00:02,703 --> 00:00:20,225
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's a question about the eight precepts, but in particular, the precepts against entertainment, like playing games, watching movies, just distraction, I suppose, distracting yourself.

2
00:00:23,121 --> 00:00:26,222
[SPEAKER_00]: Why, why is that an issue?

3
00:00:26,242 --> 00:00:36,066
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, simple game, kind of, just you're feeling under pressure, you're keeping the precepts and you just want to relax.

4
00:00:36,906 --> 00:00:37,147
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

5
00:00:38,067 --> 00:00:43,089
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's an issue because you're not free from suffering yet.

6
00:00:44,930 --> 00:00:45,730
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's an issue.

7
00:00:47,651 --> 00:00:54,315
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were to be free from suffering, then yes, there are those things that could be deemed neutral in and of themselves.

8
00:00:54,576 --> 00:01:04,902
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, oh, it's just... And that's why people would often, like, trying to reason, kind of, divorced from their situation, and look at it from its own, it's like, well, it's not a big deal.

9
00:01:05,022 --> 00:01:06,564
[SPEAKER_01]: And yeah, factually, it's not a big deal.

10
00:01:07,144 --> 00:01:09,986
[SPEAKER_01]: But, paired with your non-freedom from suffering,

11
00:01:10,878 --> 00:01:20,205
[SPEAKER_01]: Those things would, then, result in greed, aversion, delusion, that you're not free from, infestering, increasing.

12
00:01:20,805 --> 00:01:31,173
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were free from those things, then, yes, those neutral activities, even entertainment, would not cause that, because you would have removed it.

13
00:01:31,393 --> 00:01:38,438
[SPEAKER_01]: You would have basically removed the unwholesome roots that are still present in you.

14
00:01:39,883 --> 00:01:52,190
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why you need to renounce those activities as well, until you're free from suffering, until you have uprooted the cause of all problems, so to speak.

15
00:01:53,170 --> 00:02:07,798
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, yeah, if you choose to whatever, for whatever reason, engage with something that might be deemed entertainment externally, well, it won't be an entertainment for you, because the nature of entertainment is the nature of distraction.

16
00:02:08,453 --> 00:02:14,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Distraction from the present situation, that is bothering, that is oppressing, that you are not free from internally.

17
00:02:16,256 --> 00:02:21,939
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, if you just judge it by the external activity on paper, it's always nothing to it, because there isn't anything to it.

18
00:02:22,019 --> 00:02:24,160
[SPEAKER_01]: But why is it being done?

19
00:02:25,100 --> 00:02:26,521
[SPEAKER_01]: What is your intention behind it?

20
00:02:26,541 --> 00:02:31,443
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you realize, oh, yeah, now there's a whole world of difference.

21
00:02:31,643 --> 00:02:35,205
[SPEAKER_00]: It seems to me, well, it is you feeling

22
00:02:37,432 --> 00:02:40,855
[SPEAKER_00]: Displeasure, feeling boredom, and you want to get rid of that.

23
00:02:40,875 --> 00:02:41,456
[SPEAKER_01]: It has to be.

24
00:02:41,496 --> 00:02:41,836
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

25
00:02:41,876 --> 00:02:46,840
[SPEAKER_01]: Because those are things, for as long as you're not free from suffering, means those things are still going to be pressuring you.

26
00:02:47,560 --> 00:02:47,881
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know.

27
00:02:47,901 --> 00:02:53,385
[SPEAKER_01]: Think about, like, you just, I don't know, put a blanket over a grass, and it's just a blanket on the grass.

28
00:02:53,465 --> 00:02:54,446
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the problem with it?

29
00:02:54,566 --> 00:02:55,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, what's underneath the blanket?

30
00:02:57,789 --> 00:03:00,991
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's like these weeds and roots, they will now grow even more.

31
00:03:02,712 --> 00:03:05,555
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you remove it, expose it to the sun, they'll dry up and die.

32
00:03:06,402 --> 00:03:09,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, put all blankets you want on the ground.

33
00:03:09,751 --> 00:03:13,622
[SPEAKER_01]: But not before you would have got rid of the stuff that needs to be needs to be dealt with.

34
00:03:15,330 --> 00:03:19,974
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, why would you... as you said, why would you... what's the reason for distracting?

35
00:03:20,014 --> 00:03:22,215
[SPEAKER_00]: What would be the reason for, example, watching a movie?

36
00:03:22,816 --> 00:03:30,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes it can even be just a habit for people, even if it's not necessarily like fully defined pressure to distract myself.

37
00:03:30,881 --> 00:03:32,543
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just careless habit.

38
00:03:32,823 --> 00:03:34,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Just not even think about it.

39
00:03:34,624 --> 00:03:35,865
[SPEAKER_00]: In general, it's like an escape.

40
00:03:36,725 --> 00:03:37,786
[SPEAKER_01]: It is an escape, yeah.

41
00:03:38,927 --> 00:03:40,328
[SPEAKER_01]: But, again, like...

42
00:03:42,766 --> 00:03:47,069
[SPEAKER_01]: Even an escape in and of itself, it's not a problem, if there is nothing you're trying to escape.

43
00:03:48,610 --> 00:03:48,770
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

44
00:03:49,291 --> 00:03:54,274
[SPEAKER_01]: But, you won't be in a position to be not trying to escape anything, i.e.

45
00:03:54,515 --> 00:03:58,678
[SPEAKER_01]: freed, if you would not have stopped all those activities beforehand.

46
00:03:58,738 --> 00:04:11,627
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as I said, for as long as greed, aversion, delusion are still there underlying your experience, those escapes would serve to... to... to... would provide food for those... for those unwholesome roots, would feed them.

47
00:04:12,707 --> 00:04:13,847
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why they are unwholesome.

48
00:04:14,348 --> 00:04:25,072
[SPEAKER_01]: But if, say, Arahant has uprooted all these things from his from his experience as a whole, then, entertainment, there is nothing to feed.

49
00:04:25,452 --> 00:04:28,113
[SPEAKER_01]: So... and then, exactly, how much would then he enjoy it?

50
00:04:28,353 --> 00:04:32,575
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's a question of individual person, I suppose.

51
00:04:32,675 --> 00:04:38,857
[SPEAKER_01]: But... because the reason for enjoyment is usually exactly that self-forgetfulness and disappearance from the current

52
00:04:41,552 --> 00:04:51,965
[SPEAKER_01]: State of your, of your kind of emotional state, feeling, pressure, boredom, all these things, they could be there, simply taking a break from yourself, basically.

53
00:04:52,165 --> 00:04:53,227
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what it translates to.

54
00:04:57,603 --> 00:05:10,470
[SPEAKER_00]: That whole entertainment industry is like one of the biggest industries, you know, because that's... because everybody... everyone's suffering, and it's like that... it's just such an easy way to... to go to... to escape your reality.

55
00:05:11,210 --> 00:05:13,591
[SPEAKER_00]: It's built on your suffering, basically.

56
00:05:14,032 --> 00:05:24,017
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because, like, if you... if you... and you realize how much people would be dependent on it, because if you were to just stop, for example, entertainment, the pressure would increase.

57
00:05:27,291 --> 00:05:27,611
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

58
00:05:27,691 --> 00:05:32,813
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if you're not... for as long as you're not stopping it, it might not seem like a big deal.

59
00:05:33,933 --> 00:05:45,717
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, if you choose to stop it, just as an experiment, then you realize, oh, so I just have to spend the entire day without forgetting about myself through these activities.

60
00:05:45,757 --> 00:05:47,417
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize, oh, that's the unpleasant part.

61
00:05:47,758 --> 00:05:48,638
[SPEAKER_01]: Just being with yourself.

62
00:05:49,658 --> 00:05:54,900
[SPEAKER_01]: Because why is it, as I ask often, why is it just sitting with yourself, doing nothing,

63
00:05:55,752 --> 00:05:56,733
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that unpleasant?

64
00:05:57,274 --> 00:06:03,660
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that kind of something that people would be resisting by default?

65
00:06:05,001 --> 00:06:05,802
[SPEAKER_00]: No, just doing nothing.

66
00:06:06,522 --> 00:06:07,483
[SPEAKER_01]: Not distracting myself.

67
00:06:09,045 --> 00:06:11,227
[SPEAKER_01]: Not trying to, you know, get rid of anything.

68
00:06:11,407 --> 00:06:15,231
[SPEAKER_01]: Not trying to... And yet, it's like it's the worst thing.

69
00:06:15,972 --> 00:06:17,893
[SPEAKER_00]: You ask yourself these things and you realize, yeah, yeah.

70
00:06:19,375 --> 00:06:20,456
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not even unpleasant.

71
00:06:21,517 --> 00:06:25,138
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not even like that you... that you really hate or something.

72
00:06:25,158 --> 00:06:28,758
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just like, by default, you want to look away from yourself.

73
00:06:30,179 --> 00:06:32,099
[SPEAKER_01]: By default, you want to be taken elsewhere.

74
00:06:33,759 --> 00:06:38,160
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's a habit that then festers and proliferates and you become more and more dependent on that.

75
00:06:42,361 --> 00:06:47,062
[SPEAKER_01]: And, as any other habit, you can undo it by not providing fuel for it.

76
00:06:47,912 --> 00:07:04,816
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by reducing the distraction and entertainment and giving it up, you are bringing yourself back to yourself, in a sense of like, okay, so now I'm with myself and I don't need to make some extra crazy determined effort to be present in the moment with myself.

77
00:07:04,936 --> 00:07:09,357
[SPEAKER_01]: All I need to do is not distract myself from myself and I am present in the moment with myself.

78
00:07:10,620 --> 00:07:19,876
[SPEAKER_01]: So, mindfulness and awareness will be there, because you're not basically hindering it with all these habitual actions of... of... towards entertainment and delusion and distraction.

